WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:08.300
Every action the robot takes gives the world a new answer.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.500
Today's models can see the frames, but fail to read the changes.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:21.500
How does a baby learn space? By moving, using its hands, and interacting with the world.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:31.500
We use actions to understand causal change and why the world changes.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.500
One interaction naturally connects three things: observation, action, and a new observation.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:49.500
Three stages: passive world changes, active self-motion, and long-trajectory integration.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.500
108K verifiable interaction QA pairs.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:03.500
Two-stage training: SFT learns local transitions, and on-policy distillation connects them into complete states.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:12.500
A 32-frame trajectory: how far did the camera move?

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.500
Read local displacements segment by segment, then accumulate them into a complete path: 20 m traveled with only 2 m between the endpoints.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:22.000
A different question: what is the motion sequence of the complete trajectory?

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:31.500
Forward, turn left, forward, turn left, forward: five transitions form a complete spatial state.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:38.500
One interaction becomes the capability for the next.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.408
Understand the world through interaction, and keep improving through interaction.
